/-!
# `S(3,2) ≤ 6`: six multipliers hit every base-3 pair 🧵

The hitting-set search (`experiments/hitting_set_search.py`,
`docs/hitting-set-invariant-2026-09-13.md` row `(3,2)`) found the six multipliers
`{1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8}`; the emitter `experiments/adder_baseg_emit.py h32` produced
one certificate per base-3 word of length two
(`experiments/certs/adder_cert_h32_w{00..22}.json`), Python-verified.  The signed
base-`g` engine `signed_engine_g_single` turns them into

* `hitting_3_2_six` : `S(3,2) ≤ 6`, hitting set `{1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8}`.

**Scale, and why `native_decide`.**  The ambient state space of a `gfamPred`
family is the product `∏ᵢ mᵢ · g^(ℓ−1)` over its channels, so six channels at
base `3` with `ℓ = 2` sit at `2240 · 3^6 = 1632960` states — two orders of
magnitude past the `9360` of `HittingSetBase7.lean`, where a single
whole-certificate kernel `decide` already cost eleven minutes and ten gigabytes.
Chunked kernel `decide` is therefore out of reach here and each certificate is a
`native_decide` (this repo is at the formalize tier; the `#print axioms`
footprint of every theorem below carries the per-site `ofReduceBool` axiom).
The `live` / `rho` / `omega` / `forced` tables are the sorted-array binary-search
tables of `HittingSetSparse.lean`, not association lists: at this ambient size a
linear `List.lookup` over the `6685` ranked dead states would be `10^10`
comparisons.  Each certificate checks in about `90` seconds.

No subset of the six multipliers suffices: a scan over all `2^6` subfamilies
(2026-09-16) found a collapsing certificate for only four of the nine words
(`(0,2)` and `(2,0)` collapse on `{1,2,4,8}`, `(1,0)` and `(1,2)` on
`{1,2,4,5,7}`), so the full product is genuinely needed for the other five.

Nothing here claims the lower bound `S(3,2) ≥ 6`.
-/

